{"command": "cat /testbed/specs/001-transactions/spec.md", "exit_code": 0, "stderr": "", "stdout": "# Feature Specification: Tagged Cache (TaggedCache)\n\n**Feature Branch**: `001-transactions`\n\n**Created**: 2026-10-17\n\n**Status**: Draft\n\n**Input**: User description: \"Build cachetools.tagged.TaggedCache(maxsize), using the existing cachetools.LRUCache for bounded storage and preserving all existing cachetools APIs. Milestone 1 (R01-R03) retained; Milestone 2 adds R04-R06; Claim-A candidate extensions (put_no_recency_refresh 2026-09-21; resize expiry preservation 2026-09-22) recorded.\"\n\n## User Scenarios & Testing *(mandatory)*\n\n### User Story 1 - Store and retrieve tagged entries (Priority: P1)\n\nA developer creates a bounded cache that associates each stored value with a\nset of string tags. They insert entries, retrieve values, and rely on\nisolation from the originals.\n\n**Why this priority**: Core value of the feature; without store/retrieve the\ntype is useless. This is the MVP slice.\n\n**Independent Test**: Create the cache with a small capacity, insert one\nentry with tags, read it back, and confirm the returned value equals the\nstored value but is a separate object (mutating the returned value or the\noriginal does not affect the other). Also confirm a missing key raises\n`KeyError`.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** an empty cache, **When** `put(key, value, tags)` is called with\n   a finite iterable of string tags, **Then** the entry is stored and\n   `get(key)` returns a value equal to the input value.\n2. **Given** a stored entry whose value is a mutable object, **When**\n   `get(key)` is called, **Then** the returned object is a detached deep\ncopy: mutating it (and mutating the original after re-put) does not affect\n   the cached or returned counterpart.\n3. **Given** a cache, **When** `get(missing_key)` is called, **Then**\n   `KeyError` is raised and nothing else changes.\n4. **Given** a cache, **When** `put` is called with a bare string as\n   `tags`, or with a tag entry that is not a string, **Then** `ValueError`\nis raised and the cache state (entries, tags, recency) is unchanged.\n5. **Given** duplicate tags in the iterable, **When** `put` is called,\n   **Then** the entry is stored with each tag only once.\n\n---\n\n### User Story 2 - Invalidate by tag and evict with clean state (Priority: P2)\n\nA developer removes all entries carrying a tag in one call and relies on\nbounded eviction to never leak stale tag memberships.\n\n**Why this priority**: Tag-based bulk removal is the distinguishing\ncapability over the plain bounded cache; eviction hygiene is required for\ncorrectness under capacity pressure.\n\n**Independent Test**: Fill a small cache, call `invalidate(tag)`, and verify\nthe returned count and that exactly the tagged entries are gone. Then force\neviction by overfilling, re-put the evicted key with new tags, and verify\nthe old tags no longer match it.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** entries with and without tag `t`, **When** `invalidate(t)` is\n   called, **Then** exactly the entries carrying `t` are removed and the\n   return value equals the number removed.\n2. **Given** an existing key stored with old tags, **When** `put` is called\n   for the same key with new tags, **Then** the old tag memberships are\n   removed (invalidating an old tag no longer removes the entry) and the\n   value is replaced.\n3. **Given** a full cache, **When** a new entry is inserted and the\n   least-recently-used entry is evicted, **Then** the evicted key's tag\n   memberships no longer affect later reuse of that same key.\n4. **Given** a cached entry, **When** `get(key)` is called, **Then** the\n   entry becomes the most-recently-used for eviction order.\n5. **Given** any sequence of operations, **When** `len(cache)` is called,\n   **Then** it equals the number of live entries (never counting evicted or\n   invalidated entries).\n\n---\n\n### User Story 3 - Safe construction and replacement (Priority: P3)\n\nA developer constructs the cache with a capacity and expects invalid input\nto fail fast and atomically.\n\n**Why this priority**: Guard rails that prevent silent corruption; important\nbut secondary to the primary store/retrieve/invalidate flows.\n\n**Independent Test**: Attempt construction with invalid capacities and\nattempt a replacement with invalid tags; verify errors are raised and\nexisting state is untouched.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** capacity input that is not a positive integer (zero, negative,\n   non-integer, or a boolean), **When** the cache is constructed, **Then**\n   `ValueError` is raised.\n2. **Given** a stored entry, **When** `put` for the same key is called with\n   invalid tags, **Then** the old value, old tag memberships, and old\n   recency position are all unchanged.\n3. **Given** any value object, **When** it is stored, **Then** it consumes\n   exactly one capacity slot regardless of its size or complexity.\n\n---\n\n### User Story 4 - Bulk invalidation with any/all semantics (Priority: P2)\n\nA developer removes many entries at once by several tags, choosing whether\nan entry must carry *any* of the tags or *all* of them, and gets back the\nnumber of distinct entries removed.\n\n**Why this priority**: Bulk tag operations are the main scaling path for\ntag-based cleanup; they build directly on the P1/P2 tag machinery.\n\n**Independent Test**: Seed entries with overlapping tag sets, call the\nbulk-invalidation method in each mode, and verify exactly the expected\nentries are gone and the returned count equals the number of distinct\nremoved keys.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** entries tagged {a}, {b}, and {a,b}, **When** bulk\n   invalidation is requested with tags (a, b) in mode `any`, **Then** all\n   three entries are removed and the return value is 3.\n2. **Given** the same entries, **When** bulk invalidation is requested\n   with tags (a, b) in mode `all`, **Then** only the entry tagged {a,b}\nis removed and the return value is 1.\n3. **Given** any cache state, **When** bulk invalidation is requested with\n   an empty tag collection (empty list, empty tuple, or exhausted\ngenerator), **Then** zero entries are removed in either mode and the\n   return value is 0 (explicitly NOT the old design-note behavior of\n   matching every key in `all` mode).\n4. **Given** a cache state, **When** bulk invalidation is requested with an\n   invalid mode (anything other than `any` or `all`) or invalid tags (bare\n   string, non-iterable, non-string element), **Then** `ValueError` is\n   raised and no entry is removed.\n5. **Given** a single-tag request, **When** bulk invalidation is called\n   with one tag, **Then** the result is identical to the single-tag\n   `invalidate(tag)` (compatibility preserved).\n\n---\n\n### User Story 5 - Resize capacity safely (Priority: P3)\n\nA developer changes the cache capacity at runtime and expects surviving\nentries to keep their order and state; a failed resize must change\nnothing.\n\n**Why this priority**: Runtime capacity tuning is a convenience/correctness\nguard rail; it depends on the P1 entry model and P2 LRU order.\n\n**Independent Test**: Create a cache, populate it, shrink and grow it,\nverify survivors, LRU order, and that an invalid resize leaves the cache\nbyte-identical.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** a cache with capacity 5 and 3 entries, **When** it is resized\n   to 2, **Then** the two least-recently-used entries are evicted, the\n   remaining entry order is preserved, and the capacity now reports 2.\n2. **Given** a cache with capacity 2 and 2 entries, **When** it is resized\n   to 5, **Then** no entry is evicted and the capacity now reports 5.\n3. **Given** any cache state, **When** a resize is requested with an\n   invalid capacity (non-integer, boolean, zero, negative), **Then**\n   `ValueError` is raised and all entries, tags, recency order, and the\n   previous capacity are unchanged.\n4. **Given** a cache that has entries, **When** it is shrunk to a smaller\n   capacity, **Then** evicted entries' tag memberships are removed exactly\n   as with LRU eviction (no stale tags).\n5. **Given** a surviving entry across a resize, **When** the resize\n   completes, **Then** the entry's stored state is preserved as-is (no\n   re-copying, no expiry recomputation — see Assumptions for the expiry\n   clarification note).\n\n---\n\n### Edge Cases\n\n- Bare string passed as `tags` (must be rejected, not treated as an\n  iterable of characters).\n- Tag iterable containing a non-string entry (rejected before any state\n  change).\n- Duplicate tags in one `put` call (stored once).\n- Replacing a key with the same value but different tags (old memberships\n  removed; recency refreshed).\n- Replacing a key with invalid tags after a prior valid put (state fully\n  preserved, including recency).\n- Eviction of a key, then re-put of the same key (no stale tag\n  memberships from the evicted incarnation).\n- `get` on a key that was invalidated or evicted (raises `KeyError`).\n- Capacity of 1 (every put evicts the previous entry).\n- `maxsize` given as `True`/`False` (rejected: booleans are excluded).\n- Mutating a returned deep copy or the original object (no cross-talk with\n  the cache).\n\n- Bulk invalidation with duplicate tags in the request (deduplicated\n  before matching; count is distinct removed keys).\n- Bulk invalidation `all` mode with an empty tag set (removes zero,\n  never every key — supersedes the old design note).\n- Bulk invalidation with a generator tag source (consumed exactly once;\n  on validation failure the cache is unchanged).\n- Resize to the same capacity (no-op; state unchanged).\n- Resize shrinking to exactly the current entry count (no eviction).\n- Resize with boolean/zero/negative/non-int capacity (rejected, state\n  preserved).\n- Growing then shrinking back (order and entries survive the round trip).\n## Requirements *(mandatory)*\n\n### Functional Requirements\n\n- **FR-001**: The system MUST expose a new `TaggedCache` type in\n  `cachetools.tagged` (module `src/cachetools/tagged.py`) constructed with a\n  single `maxsize` argument (R01/R03 scope).\n- **FR-002**: `put(key, value, tags=())` MUST insert or replace an entry;\n  keys MUST be hashable; values MUST be copied on input so later mutation\n  of the caller's object does not change the stored value (R01).\n- **FR-003**: `get(key)` MUST return a detached deep copy of the stored\n  value, or raise `KeyError` for a missing key; it MUST refresh the\n  entry's recency (R01/R02).\n- **FR-004**: `tags` MUST be a finite iterable of strings; the system MUST\n  deduplicate tags per entry; a bare string MUST be rejected as `tags` and\n  any non-string tag entry MUST raise `ValueError`, in both cases before\n  any existing state is changed (R01).\n- **FR-005**: `invalidate(tag)` MUST remove exactly the currently cached\n  entries carrying that tag and return the number removed (R02).\n- **FR-006**: Replacing an existing key MUST remove its old tag\n  memberships (R02).\n- **FR-007**: LRU eviction MUST remove the evicted entry's tag\n  memberships so later reuse of the same key is not affected by stale\n  state (R02).\n- **FR-008**: `get` MUST refresh LRU recency of the accessed entry (R02).\n- **FR-009**: `len(cache)` MUST count only live entries (R02).\n- **FR-010**: `maxsize` MUST be a positive integer; booleans MUST be\n  excluded; invalid values MUST raise `ValueError` (R03).\n- **FR-011**: A replacement `put` with invalid input MUST leave the old\n  value, old tags, and old recency unchanged (R03).\n- **FR-012**: Every stored value MUST cost exactly one capacity slot,\n  regardless of object size (R03).\n- **FR-013**: All existing `cachetools` public APIs MUST remain importable\n- **FR-014**: `invalidate_many(tags, mode='any')` MUST remove entries\n  by tag set: mode `any` removes entries carrying at least one requested\n  tag (union); mode `all` removes entries carrying every requested tag;\n  it MUST return the number of distinct removed keys (R04).\n- **FR-015**: An empty requested tag collection MUST remove zero entries\n  in either mode; an invalid `mode` or invalid `tags` MUST raise\n  `ValueError` before any mutation (R04).\n- **FR-016**: `invalidate(tag)` MUST remain fully compatible with\n  `invalidate_many` for single-tag requests (R04).\n- **FR-017**: `resize(maxsize)` MUST validate the capacity first (same\n  rule as FR-010); a failed resize MUST preserve all state (entries,\n  tags, recency order, current capacity); a successful shrink MUST evict\n  least-recently-used entries; a successful grow MUST evict nothing;\n  surviving entries MUST keep their LRU order; the `maxsize` property\n  MUST report the current capacity after a successful resize (R05).\n- **FR-018**: Tag iterables (including generators) MUST be consumed\n  exactly once per call; on validation failure the cache MUST be\n  unchanged (R06).\n- **FR-019**: Returned values MUST remain detached across repeated\n  `get`s and `put`s, including nested mutable structures (R06).\n  and behaviorally unchanged; this feature is additive only (milestone\n  constraint; Constitution Principle I).\n\n### Key Entities\n\n- **Cache entry**: one key, one stored (copied) value, a deduplicated set\n  of string tags, and a recency position among live entries.\n- **Tag**: a string label shared by zero or more live entries; the unit\n  for bulk invalidation.\n- **Capacity**: the positive-integer bound on the number of live entries;\n  each entry costs one slot.\n\n## Success Criteria *(mandatory)*\n\n### Measurable Outcomes\n\n- **SC-001**: 100% of the public acceptance suite (`acceptance_public.py`)\n  passes unmodified.\n- **SC-002**: 100% of the functional requirements FR-001..FR-019 are\n  covered by at least one passing test with observed output.\n- **SC-003**: Zero regressions: all pre-existing `cachetools` public APIs\n  behave identically before and after the change (verified by the existing\n  upstream test suite, unmodified).\n- **SC-004**: Every invalid-input case in the specification (bare string\n  tags, non-string tag entries, invalid `maxsize`, invalid replacement)\n  raises the specified error and leaves observable state byte-identical to\n  the pre-call state.\n- **SC-005**: Under repeated store/retrieve/invalidate/evict cycles on a\n  small capacity, `len(cache)` always equals the true number of live\n  entries and `invalidate` counts always equal the number of entries\n  actually removed.\n\n- **SC-006**: Bulk invalidation and resize boundary cases (empty tag\n  set in both modes, invalid mode, invalid capacity, shrink/grow order\n  preservation) each have a passing test with observed output, and the\n  superseded design-note behaviors (empty `all` matches every key;\n  resize flushes the cache) are demonstrably NOT implemented.## Assumptions\n\n- The feature is a library addition to the existing `cachetools` package;\n  no CLI, service, or UI is involved.\n- \"Deep copy\" means the returned value is fully detached from the stored\n  value (nested mutable structures included), consistent with standard\n  deep-copy semantics for the value's type.\n- Recency order (which entry is least-recently-used) is an observable\n  behavior through eviction order and replacement behavior, not exposed as\n  a separate query API.\n- Tag strings are compared by value; no normalization (case, whitespace)\nis applied.\n- `put` returns nothing significant to callers (no return-value contract\n  is specified).\n- Only the new module `src/cachetools/tagged.py` and new self-authored\n  tests are added; upstream tests and `acceptance_public.py` are never\n  modified.\n- **Claim-A candidate extension (variant `put_no_recency_refresh`,\n- **Milestone 2 supersession (R04/R05)**: an old generated design\n  note claimed \"empty all-tags matches every key and resizing may flush\n  the cache\". That prose is superseded by R04 (empty requested tags\n  remove zero entries in either mode) and R05 (resize preserves\n  surviving entries; a failed resize preserves all state). The spec and\n  tests follow R04/R05, not the old note.\n- **Claim-A candidate extension, round 2 (2026-09-22) — R05\n  clarification on expiry**: the extension text references expiry as\n  entry state (\"R07/R08 define the expiry as entry state\"), but no\n  R07/R08 expiry requirement exists in the current frozen requirements\n  (R01-R06 only). Resolution for this milestone: entries have NO expiry\n  attribute; therefore \"preserving absolute expiry across resize\" is\n  vacuously satisfied by preserving the entry's stored state exactly as-is (no re-copying of value identity beyond the existing deep-copy\n  contract, no recomputation of any time-based state). If a future\n  milestone adds R07/R08 (TTL/expiry), this assumption MUST be revisited\n  and resize MUST then preserve each surviving entry's absolute expiry\n  timestamp rather than dropping or recomputing it. Tracked as an open\n  item for clarify/plan.\n  2026-09-21)**: recorded as an explicit candidate extension to R02 —\n  overwriting an existing key via `put` refreshes its LRU recency, making\n  it the most-recently-used entry. It is NOT silently merged into the base\n  requirements; the base spec already requires recency refresh on `get`\n  (FR-008) and replacement semantics (FR-006). Whether replacement via\n  `put` also refreshes recency is flagged for the clarify/plan phase to\n  resolve against the variant name, which suggests the candidate argues the\n  opposite (no refresh on put). This is the single open item carried\n  forward.\n"}